# Day 2 

**Task:** Solve logical programming problems. Create reusable JavaScript functions.

## What I actually did

Two files again, same idea as yesterday one for practice, one for stuff I'll actually keep.

**`js/logic-exercises.js`** is the practice one. If/else logic for checking a launch status, a switch statement for turning an agency name into a short code, a fake countdown using a `for` loop, a fuel-draining example using `while` just to see the difference between the two, and a quick example of `forEach` to actually use a callback instead of just reading about what one is.

**`js/mission-functions.js`** is the one that matters. These are actual functions built for the real project:

- `getStatusLabel()` — turns "go" into "Go", "review" into "Under Review", that kind of thing
- `formatLaunchDate()` — turns "2026-09-14" into "Sep 14, 2026" so it's actually readable
- `filterLaunchesByStatus()` — pulls out only the launches matching one status, for a filter feature later
- `sortLaunchesByDate()` — sorts launches soonest-first without messing up the original list
- `buildAlertMessage()` — puts together the confirmation text for the sign-up form

I tested every one of these with `console.log` before considering it done. None of them touch the actual webpage yet — no DOM, no click handlers, nothing visible changes. That's on purpose, same reasoning as always: prove the logic works on its own first, wire it into the page later.

## Login, sign up, and forgot password pages

Also added three new pages today `login.html`, `signup.html`, and `forgot-password.html` plus a shared `auth.css` for styling them consistently with the rest of the site.

Being honest about what this actually is: these are **frontend only**. There's no backend, no database, no real authentication happening. Submitting the login form doesn't check anything against a real account because there isn't one yet. I added a small note directly on each page saying exactly that, instead of letting it look like it works when it doesn't.

What I did build for real:
- A login page with email, password, a "remember me" checkbox, and a link to forgot password
- A sign up page with name, email, password, and confirm password, plus a little password-strength bar (visual only right now, not wired to anything)
- A forgot password page that just asks for an email and explains what a reset link would normally do
- All three share one card-style layout so they feel like the same product instead of three random forms
- Added a "Sign In" link to the main site's nav bar so these pages are actually reachable, including inside the mobile menu

This is technically ahead of where the curriculum has me login/register interfaces are officially a Week 5 topic, once real form validation and authentication concepts get covered. I built the frontend for it now mostly because it was asked for directly, but I'm keeping the scope honest: this is UI and layout practice, not working authentication. The real validation logic and any backend connection will come later when I actually know how to do it properly, instead of faking it now with something that looks functional but isn't.
